+## Husky: session-start relocation
+
+The `husky` adapter uses **session-start-relocation**, rather than a PATH
+shim: npm can invoke Husky's local binary directly. Detection metadata names
+the `.husky` directory and the `husky` development dependency; an unrelated
+`package.json` alone does not establish Husky use. The supported path is exactly
+`.husky/_` (Husky 9). Custom installation directories are reported, not rewritten.
+
+With the whole-user dispatcher enabled, SessionStart in a main checkout moves
+the repository's plain `core.hooksPath = .husky/_` to the single repository-local
+`magpie.localHooksPath` key in one atomic config rewrite. The dispatcher then
+runs the actual `<worktree>/.husky/_/<hook-name>` wrapper, preserving arguments,
+standard input and the hook's failure status. This saved path replaces the
+ordinary `.git/hooks` lookup, matching Git's single hooks-directory semantics.
+The framework's post-checkout synchronization still runs first.
+
+An npm install **during the session** can restore `core.hooksPath`, bypassing
+the dispatcher until the next SessionStart. Husky's checks still run in that
+window, but the dispatcher's host-only cache selection and post-checkout
+synchronization do not. Start a new session after an install before relying on
+those protections. This adapter does not intercept every npm or Git command.
+
+Main-checkout reconciliation prepares the shared config for linked worktrees;
+each linked worktree resolves its own `.husky/_` directory. A session started
+only in a linked worktree never writes through its sandbox-writable `.git`
+pointer: inspect hooks paths and start a session in the main checkout first.
+`extensions.worktreeConfig`/`config.worktree`, custom paths, duplicate saved
+keys and unparseable/included configurations are unsupported and reported.
+
+Outside the sandbox, Husky's `init.sh` must be protected from sandbox writes.
+The dispatcher checks the lexical path and resolved existing ancestors even
+when the file is missing. It considers user, project, local and managed
+settings, worktrees, cache and temporary roots; unknown write-grant syntax
+fails closed. A missing file in a writable directory is not safe. The committed
+`Edit(~/.config/husky/**)` deny provides defense in depth, not proof that a
+custom XDG configuration directory or a broader operator grant is safe.
+Unsafe hooks/init locations are refused with a sanitized diagnostic; operator
+files and configuration are not deleted or rewritten by this check.
+Sandboxed hooks keep their existing init behavior. Husky is not disabled.
+
+Changing registry strategies or detection fields requires updating their
+validation and regression tests in `tools/dev/tests/test_sync_hook_managers.py`.
+The official sync script generates blocks; the SessionStart code and dispatcher
+implement relocation. Regenerate the setup fingerprint after changing them.
+
+
+The host init check treats `Edit` permission paths separately from filesystem
+write paths: a single leading slash in an Edit rule is settings-source-relative,
+while `//` is absolute. Filesystem write entries use ordinary absolute paths;
+user-relative entries are based at the user configuration directory. See the
+[permission path rules](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions#how-path-patterns-match)
+and [sandbox path rules](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings-reference#sandbox-path-prefixes).
+Unknown/complex patterns fail closed. Additional-directory entries must use an
+absolute or home-relative spelling for this adapter; ambiguous relative entries
+are refused rather than guessed. Configurations supplied only through a live
+CLI/SDK override, server policy or MDM are not proven by reading the local JSON
+files; do not treat this check as proof of their effective sandbox grants.
+
+
+### Husky host-init protection boundary
+
+The host-side `init.sh` guard covers the framework's configured write grants,
+readable user settings, project/local settings for every worktree, and the
+documented managed JSON files and drop-ins. It does not reconstruct grants
+supplied only through CLI/SDK additions or non-file server/MDM policy. Operators
+who widen write access through those unsupported modes are outside this
+protection, as with the existing host-only manager-home rule. This is not a
+claim of universal effective-settings inspection.
+
+Unknown grant forms fail closed. In particular, hooks are refused while a
+relative `additionalDirectories` entry exists: spell it as an absolute or
+home-relative directory. Relative Edit paths with `..`, and source-relative
+Edit paths in managed settings whose anchor cannot be established, are refused.
+The check reads at most 1 MiB per settings file and 8 MiB in total.
+
+The committed `Edit(~/.config/husky/**)` deny is a second layer for the default
+location. Claude Code documents [deny-before-allow precedence across settings
+scopes](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions#settings-precedence) and
+[conversion of Edit denies into the sandbox filesystem deny list](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings-reference#sandboxfilesystem).
+That protects the default path when these rules are loaded and the documented
+sandbox filesystem enforcement is enabled. It does not establish protection for
+arbitrary XDG paths, disabled filesystem isolation or unseen policy sources.
